## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to reason about cascading failures in a real-world, multi-region microservices architecture. It evaluates understanding of both preventive controls (rate limits, circuit breakers) and reactive strategies (cache bypass, warmup) with measurable SLOs and fast rollback capabilities.

## Key Concepts

- Per-endpoint rate limiting and circuit breakers for load control
- Cache warmup and controlled rollouts for spike preparation
- Backpressure and producer throttling to regulate traffic flow
- Eviction tuning and failover paths for resilience
- Automated rollback and SLO-driven incident response

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple token bucket limiter (conceptual)
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(capacity, refillRate) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.tokens = capacity;
    this.refillRate = refillRate;
    this.lastRefill = Date.now();
  }

  allowRequest() {
    this.refill();
    if (this.tokens >= 1) {
      this.tokens -= 1;
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  }

  refill() {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = (now - this.lastRefill) / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, this.tokens + elapsed * this.refillRate);
    this.lastRefill = now;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes ability to design scalable, tenant-aware incident response and degradation at scale. It tests how candidates balance automated safeguards with precise human oversight and how runbooks integrate metrics, backfills, and recovery verification.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-region, multi-tenant SLAs
- Degradation strategies and traffic shaping
- Cache strategy and data consistency during outages
- Canary testing, rollback criteria, post-incident validation

## Code Example
